00:01:10.840 --> 00:01:12.380
leads on the engineering side.

00:01:12.380 --> 00:01:14.847
So that's a little
bit my background.

00:01:14.847 --> 00:01:16.930
Prior to Google, though,
I think it's interesting,

00:01:16.930 --> 00:01:20.270
though, that I was a very
traditional network engineering

00:01:20.270 --> 00:01:20.770
guy.

00:01:20.770 --> 00:01:23.620
So I worked for a
large telco company.

00:01:23.620 --> 00:01:26.110
And I dealt with
the lower layers

00:01:26.110 --> 00:01:30.530
of the stack, like I say,
very traditional networking.

00:01:30.530 --> 00:01:33.160
So what I'm going to talk
about today is something

00:01:33.160 --> 00:01:35.760
of a functional shift
for myself in addition

00:01:35.760 --> 00:01:38.260
to the customers, which is why
I thought it was interesting,

00:01:38.260 --> 00:01:39.970
because it definitely
has me thinking

00:01:39.970 --> 00:01:42.250
a little bit differently
about how we build things

00:01:42.250 --> 00:01:45.520
and how the Cloud is
evolving from, say, the way

00:01:45.520 --> 00:01:47.320
we were having
conversations with customers

00:01:47.320 --> 00:01:48.670
just even a few years ago.

00:01:48.670 --> 00:01:49.545
STEPHANIE WONG: Yeah.

00:01:49.545 --> 00:01:51.610
And you've referred to
this before as network

00:01:51.610 --> 00:01:54.250
versus service-oriented model.

00:01:54.250 --> 00:01:56.660
And that's the mentality
shift that has to happen.

00:01:56.660 --> 00:01:59.140
So why has this suddenly
become so front and center

00:01:59.140 --> 00:02:00.550
for you and your customers?

00:02:00.550 --> 00:02:00.820
RYAN PRZBYL: Yeah.

00:02:00.820 --> 00:02:02.528
So this is kind of
what I was mentioning,

00:02:02.528 --> 00:02:04.670
where I see the conversation
itself changing.

00:02:04.670 --> 00:02:06.970
There's two types
of conversations

00:02:06.970 --> 00:02:09.169
I find myself having with
customers these days.

00:02:09.169 --> 00:02:12.573
So when I talk about that
network-centric model, what

00:02:12.573 --> 00:02:13.990
I'm really talking
about is, think

00:02:13.990 --> 00:02:15.730
about how we've built
traditional data

00:02:15.730 --> 00:02:18.580
centers for the past
20, 30 years that we've

00:02:18.580 --> 00:02:20.230
been building these things.

00:02:20.230 --> 00:02:21.400
It's very network-centric.

00:02:21.400 --> 00:02:24.730
And what I mean by that is we're
dealing with a lot of routing,

00:02:24.730 --> 00:02:27.430
we're dealing with a lot
of security elements.

00:02:27.430 --> 00:02:31.150
Typically, I see this with
very large enterprises, people

00:02:31.150 --> 00:02:32.892
that have tons of applications.

00:02:32.892 --> 00:02:34.600
As an example, I worked
with one customer

00:02:34.600 --> 00:02:38.440
who wanted to migrate 10,000
applications into GCP.

00:02:38.440 --> 00:02:40.450
It's a very monumental task.

00:02:40.450 --> 00:02:43.090
And one of the things
that we always look at

00:02:43.090 --> 00:02:46.160
is, how can we make it very easy
for the customer to migrate in?

00:02:46.160 --> 00:02:48.488
And a lot of times-- we use
the term forklifted right.

00:02:48.488 --> 00:02:50.530
It's sort of taking their
data center environment

00:02:50.530 --> 00:02:52.390
and replicating in the Cloud.

00:02:52.390 --> 00:02:55.042
Now, we never want to do an
apples to apples replication.

00:02:55.042 --> 00:02:56.500
We want to maybe
change some things

00:02:56.500 --> 00:02:58.167
and take advantage
of some of the things

00:02:58.167 --> 00:03:00.230
that Cloud brings to the table.

00:03:00.230 --> 00:03:02.630
But when I say like
networking-centric

00:03:02.630 --> 00:03:04.630
architectures and
network-centric conversations,

00:03:04.630 --> 00:03:05.560
it's all about that.

00:03:05.560 --> 00:03:06.730
How do I manipulate routes?

00:03:06.730 --> 00:03:09.430
How do I build this
infrastructure?

00:03:09.430 --> 00:03:11.650
And a lot of times,
the customers

00:03:11.650 --> 00:03:14.152
don't even really-- because
there are so many applications

00:03:14.152 --> 00:03:16.360
or they don't really deal
with the application teams,

00:03:16.360 --> 00:03:18.235
it's really infrastructure
engineering teams,

00:03:18.235 --> 00:03:20.830
they don't even know what's
going to come into the Cloud.

00:03:20.830 --> 00:03:23.080
Now, the other conversation
I think we have

00:03:23.080 --> 00:03:26.020
and when I use the term
service-centric model,

00:03:26.020 --> 00:03:28.480
I typically see
this with customers

00:03:28.480 --> 00:03:31.430
that were born in the Cloud
or are relatively new.

00:03:31.430 --> 00:03:34.390
They're not dealing with a whole
ton of monolithic applications

00:03:34.390 --> 00:03:35.720
on the back side.

00:03:35.720 --> 00:03:37.720
They are applications
that have been built using

00:03:37.720 --> 00:03:39.010
microservice architectures.

00:03:39.010 --> 00:03:41.530
And we're talking a
lot about services,

00:03:41.530 --> 00:03:42.825
less about networking.

00:03:42.825 --> 00:03:44.450
It's not that networking
doesn't exist.

00:03:44.450 --> 00:03:47.290
It's just the focus isn't
so much on networking,

00:03:47.290 --> 00:03:50.110
more on services
and how services

00:03:50.110 --> 00:03:51.545
operate in the environment.

00:03:51.545 --> 00:03:52.420
STEPHANIE WONG: Yeah.

00:03:52.420 --> 00:03:54.503
And this is something that
I bring up all the time

00:03:54.503 --> 00:03:56.592
in terms of different
concepts in the Cloud.

00:03:56.592 --> 00:03:59.050
And it seems like we're moving
towards this trend of higher

00:03:59.050 --> 00:04:01.840
level of abstractions, whether
we're talking about networking

00:04:01.840 --> 00:04:03.940
or we're talking
about virtualization.

00:04:03.940 --> 00:04:06.310
So you've talked about
larger enterprises

00:04:06.310 --> 00:04:08.390
with these
network-oriented teams.

00:04:08.390 --> 00:04:10.570
What's the starting
point for them

00:04:10.570 --> 00:04:14.080
versus a smaller company that
might be starting already more

00:04:14.080 --> 00:04:14.680
Cloud native?

00:04:14.680 --> 00:04:15.430
RYAN PRZBYL: Yeah.

00:04:15.430 --> 00:04:18.850
So I think when we talk about
these larger customers--

00:04:18.850 --> 00:04:20.380
I mentioned earlier data center.

00:04:20.380 --> 00:04:23.320
We use a term like DCT,
Data Center Transformation.

00:04:23.320 --> 00:04:24.850
And this is where
we're typically

00:04:24.850 --> 00:04:27.520
working with these customers
who own their own data centers.

00:04:27.520 --> 00:04:30.700
And quite frankly,
if you're a retailer

00:04:30.700 --> 00:04:34.900
or you're in some other
business that is not networking,

00:04:34.900 --> 00:04:37.583
running a data center is
not your core business.

00:04:37.583 --> 00:04:39.250
So it's kind of like
these companies are

00:04:39.250 --> 00:04:40.780
like, hey, I have to be an
expert in running a data

00:04:40.780 --> 00:04:42.640
center, yet that's
not my core business.

00:04:42.640 --> 00:04:45.340
I am a retailer
selling a product.

00:04:45.340 --> 00:04:46.960
But networking and
all the stuff that

00:04:46.960 --> 00:04:48.418
goes around operating
your business

00:04:48.418 --> 00:04:50.650
is such a big aspect
of the business

00:04:50.650 --> 00:04:52.610
that they're running
their own data centers.

00:04:52.610 --> 00:04:56.357
So when we're talking with those
customers a lot, it's about,

00:04:56.357 --> 00:04:57.940
how do they operate
that center today?

00:04:57.940 --> 00:05:01.420
And how do we create, again,
not the same exact model--

00:05:01.420 --> 00:05:04.120
but how do we take a lot of
those networking constructs

00:05:04.120 --> 00:05:07.060
and give them those same
constructs in the Cloud

00:05:07.060 --> 00:05:10.930
so that they can move a
lot of these applications?

00:05:10.930 --> 00:05:12.520
One of the challenges
that we have

00:05:12.520 --> 00:05:15.475
is when we're moving these
applications, we're trying to--

00:05:15.475 --> 00:05:17.600
I don't want to say minimize
the amount of changes.

00:05:17.600 --> 00:05:20.380
But you can imagine if
you're a company who's

00:05:20.380 --> 00:05:23.170
used to operating a
data center and you're

00:05:23.170 --> 00:05:25.418
forced to move
10,000 applications,

00:05:25.418 --> 00:05:27.460
you don't want to make a
ton of changes to things

00:05:27.460 --> 00:05:29.127
because the more stuff
you're migrating,

00:05:29.127 --> 00:05:31.720
the more things that can
break and not work and make

00:05:31.720 --> 00:05:34.480
your Cloud journey more painful.

00:05:34.480 --> 00:05:37.060
So when I think
about how we talk

00:05:37.060 --> 00:05:40.330
about a lot of
these conversations,

00:05:40.330 --> 00:05:42.160
it's really in the
framework of, help

00:05:42.160 --> 00:05:46.570
me understand how your data
center is operating today.

00:05:46.570 --> 00:05:48.820
When we're dealing with more
of a Cloud native company

00:05:48.820 --> 00:05:51.460
or a company that was
born in the last few years

00:05:51.460 --> 00:05:53.990
and has really developed
applications very differently,

00:05:53.990 --> 00:05:57.340
they're not dealing with
10,000 applications that

00:05:57.340 --> 00:05:59.740
have been built
over the 30 years,

00:05:59.740 --> 00:06:02.073
they don't operate
a data center.

00:06:02.073 --> 00:06:04.240
Just as an example, this
is a kind of a funny story.

00:06:04.240 --> 00:06:06.265
I talked with a customer
who introduced me

00:06:06.265 --> 00:06:07.390
to their network engineers.

00:06:07.390 --> 00:06:08.620
And their network
engineers told me,

00:06:08.620 --> 00:06:09.912
I've never configured a router.

00:06:09.912 --> 00:06:12.245
I don't really know a whole
lot about how routing works.

00:06:12.245 --> 00:06:13.930
Yet these are their
network engineers.

00:06:13.930 --> 00:06:16.270
But because they're
not dealing with a lot

00:06:16.270 --> 00:06:20.137
of these antiquated
older constructs,

00:06:20.137 --> 00:06:21.470
they weren't familiar with them.

00:06:21.470 --> 00:06:22.580
And they weren't as
concerned about them.

00:06:22.580 --> 00:06:24.590
So the conversations,
again, were very different.

00:06:24.590 --> 00:06:26.215
It was more about,
this is the service.

00:06:26.215 --> 00:06:28.460
This is how the service
actually operates.

00:06:28.460 --> 00:06:30.930
How do I actually build
this in the environment?

00:06:30.930 --> 00:06:32.680
And like I said, we
weren't really getting

00:06:32.680 --> 00:06:36.520
into all these complex details
about creating routing domains

00:06:36.520 --> 00:06:38.770
and overlaying very complex
security policy over that.

00:06:38.770 --> 00:06:41.463
A lot of that stuff comes
from just legacy stuff

00:06:41.463 --> 00:06:42.880
and the way customers
have thought

00:06:42.880 --> 00:06:43.960
about their data center.

00:06:43.960 --> 00:06:44.877
STEPHANIE WONG: Right.

00:06:44.877 --> 00:06:46.780
And I think traditionally,
network architects

00:06:46.780 --> 00:06:48.947
and engineers were the ones
who were handling things

00:06:48.947 --> 00:06:52.090
like routing and when
they were physical things.

00:06:52.090 --> 00:06:54.780
And so it seemed
like before, they

00:06:54.780 --> 00:06:56.530
were the ones who were
handling this while

00:06:56.530 --> 00:06:59.110
not having as much visibility
into what application

00:06:59.110 --> 00:07:01.060
developers were doing.

00:07:01.060 --> 00:07:04.150
And it kind of reminds me of how
things are starting to change.

00:07:04.150 --> 00:07:07.750
Even when it comes to
DevOps culture for example,

00:07:07.750 --> 00:07:11.230
there's more relationship
between developers

00:07:11.230 --> 00:07:12.370
and operators.

00:07:12.370 --> 00:07:15.160
So do you see that
happening with networking

00:07:15.160 --> 00:07:16.990
and the application developers?

00:07:16.990 --> 00:07:20.398
Or is it still somewhat
of a black box architects,

00:07:20.398 --> 00:07:21.940
and will it continue
to be like that?

00:07:21.940 --> 00:07:23.200
RYAN PRZBYL: Yeah.

00:07:23.200 --> 00:07:25.990
I definitely think a lot of
customers still very much--

00:07:25.990 --> 00:07:29.020
I'm going to use the term
separation of church and state.

00:07:29.020 --> 00:07:31.480
I work with a very large
company out on the West Coast.

00:07:31.480 --> 00:07:34.540
And we work with their
entire engineering team.

00:07:34.540 --> 00:07:37.398
And just as a case
in point, they

00:07:37.398 --> 00:07:39.190
don't even know what
applications are going

00:07:39.190 --> 00:07:40.430
to come into the environment.

00:07:40.430 --> 00:07:43.720
So they build everything as
a hybrid Cloud environment

00:07:43.720 --> 00:07:46.090
where they present
to their developers

00:07:46.090 --> 00:07:48.070
and application teams
like, here's what

00:07:48.070 --> 00:07:50.080
services are available in GCP.

00:07:50.080 --> 00:07:52.570
But here's what services are
available at other clouds.

00:07:52.570 --> 00:07:54.910
And they leave it up to
the application teams

00:07:54.910 --> 00:07:57.670
to decide where
they want to deploy.

00:07:57.670 --> 00:07:59.020
So it could be in GCP.

00:07:59.020 --> 00:08:00.792
Or it could be one
of our competitors.

00:08:00.792 --> 00:08:02.500
And this is becoming
more and more common

00:08:02.500 --> 00:08:05.275
too where people are wanting
to embrace a multi-Cloud sort

00:08:05.275 --> 00:08:06.410
of environment.

00:08:06.410 --> 00:08:08.800
So they're trying to
create kind of an apples

00:08:08.800 --> 00:08:11.290
to apples comparison between
these different clouds

00:08:11.290 --> 00:08:14.110
and the data center that
they run on prem so that when

00:08:14.110 --> 00:08:18.172
an application team comes in
to run something in the Cloud,

00:08:18.172 --> 00:08:20.380
they don't really care where
it runs, whether it runs

00:08:20.380 --> 00:08:24.160
in their data center on
prem, whether it runs in GCP,

00:08:24.160 --> 00:08:25.750
or runs in one of
our competitors.

00:08:25.750 --> 00:08:28.030
They leave that up to
the application team

00:08:28.030 --> 00:08:30.350
to decide what is the
best environment for them.

00:08:30.350 --> 00:08:33.770
So in that case, they are in
some ways completely isolated.

00:08:33.770 --> 00:08:35.770
And it makes it really
interesting because when

00:08:35.770 --> 00:08:38.530
we talk to them about, what is
the future workload look like

00:08:38.530 --> 00:08:41.200
and how do you plan the stuff?

00:08:41.200 --> 00:08:42.760
One, they don't even know.

00:08:42.760 --> 00:08:45.430
And two, I say this
kind of loosely,

00:08:45.430 --> 00:08:46.695
but they don't really care.

00:08:46.695 --> 00:08:48.820
It's kind of like, we just
build the infrastructure

00:08:48.820 --> 00:08:50.362
and we present all
these capabilities

00:08:50.362 --> 00:08:53.320
to our internal business units,
and they make all the decisions

00:08:53.320 --> 00:08:54.340
around this stuff.

00:08:54.340 --> 00:08:56.140
STEPHANIE WONG: Do you think
that that's the case for Cloud

00:08:56.140 --> 00:08:56.640
too?

00:08:56.640 --> 00:08:59.260
Because I'm imagining an
application developer that

00:08:59.260 --> 00:09:01.090
is working with, let's
say, Google Cloud.

00:09:01.090 --> 00:09:04.480
They do have to somewhat have
a fundamental understanding

00:09:04.480 --> 00:09:07.780
of how VPCs work
and somewhat know

00:09:07.780 --> 00:09:09.220
the operational aspects of it.

00:09:09.220 --> 00:09:11.740
Or do you still
think it is fairly

00:09:11.740 --> 00:09:13.187
segregated in that aspect?

00:09:13.187 --> 00:09:14.770
RYAN PRZBYL: Again,
I think it depends

00:09:14.770 --> 00:09:16.250
on the size of the organization.

00:09:16.250 --> 00:09:17.950
The really big
organizations with--

00:09:17.950 --> 00:09:20.020
let's say you have 500
traditional network

00:09:20.020 --> 00:09:21.340
engineers working for you.

00:09:21.340 --> 00:09:23.690
It's still a very
segregated conversation.

00:09:23.690 --> 00:09:25.300
And then I would
also say, depending

00:09:25.300 --> 00:09:27.040
on how we build
the infrastructure,

00:09:27.040 --> 00:09:29.140
the developers don't
always really even

00:09:29.140 --> 00:09:31.630
have to have a lot of
knowledge about the underlying

00:09:31.630 --> 00:09:32.710
infrastructure.

00:09:32.710 --> 00:09:36.580
If the team that we work
with does it the right way

00:09:36.580 --> 00:09:38.290
and they're
automating everything

00:09:38.290 --> 00:09:40.570
and they're using
a lot of the tools

00:09:40.570 --> 00:09:42.340
that we give them
and recommend, they

00:09:42.340 --> 00:09:45.647
can deploy the environment very
quickly and very seamlessly.

00:09:45.647 --> 00:09:47.230
And then the developers
can come along

00:09:47.230 --> 00:09:49.870
and everything is completely
transparent to them.

00:09:49.870 --> 00:09:50.950
They can build VMs.

00:09:50.950 --> 00:09:54.490
They can do what developers
want to do and not

00:09:54.490 --> 00:09:57.740
have to worry about all of
the infrastructure components.

00:09:57.740 --> 00:10:01.300
So again, if you
kind of do it right,

00:10:01.300 --> 00:10:03.100
you can have the
infrastructure team still

00:10:03.100 --> 00:10:06.100
being very walled off and have
the development team come in

00:10:06.100 --> 00:10:07.990
and do their work.

00:10:07.990 --> 00:10:10.768
But again, to your point,
the newer companies

00:10:10.768 --> 00:10:12.310
that we deal with--
when I say newer,

00:10:12.310 --> 00:10:14.290
I think about companies
that are doing

00:10:14.290 --> 00:10:16.390
service offerings in the Cloud.

00:10:16.390 --> 00:10:18.580
Maybe that's their
main business.

00:10:18.580 --> 00:10:20.260
A lot of times their
network engineers

00:10:20.260 --> 00:10:22.120
are definitely a
lot more attached

00:10:22.120 --> 00:10:24.622
at the hip with the developers
and with the services that

00:10:24.622 --> 00:10:26.080
are running because
they need to be

00:10:26.080 --> 00:10:28.450
running just a handful
of services in the Cloud

00:10:28.450 --> 00:10:29.510
environment.

00:10:29.510 --> 00:10:32.110
It's not like I said
before, an engineering

00:10:32.110 --> 00:10:34.060
team that's like, we
have 10,000 applications.

00:10:34.060 --> 00:10:36.280
They can't be like
attached at the hip

00:10:36.280 --> 00:10:38.800
with 10,000 different
developers who are working

00:10:38.800 --> 00:10:40.300
on all of this different stuff.

00:10:40.300 --> 00:10:41.750
It's just impossible.

00:10:41.750 --> 00:10:43.900
So I think a lot
of your question

00:10:43.900 --> 00:10:46.360
kind of depends on, how
big it is the organization

00:10:46.360 --> 00:10:47.440
and how much--

00:10:47.440 --> 00:10:48.970
we use the term technical debt.

00:10:48.970 --> 00:10:50.710
But how much
baggage do they have

00:10:50.710 --> 00:10:53.085
that they're trying to move
into their Cloud environment?

00:10:53.085 --> 00:10:53.960
STEPHANIE WONG: Yeah.

00:10:53.960 --> 00:10:55.930
And so let's drill down
on that a little bit

00:10:55.930 --> 00:11:00.370
because we're talking about
companies of varying sizes,

00:11:00.370 --> 00:11:02.370
backgrounds, network
topology sizes.

00:11:02.370 --> 00:11:04.600
So how does the path
differ for a company that

00:11:04.600 --> 00:11:06.610
does have 10,000
applications and more

00:11:06.610 --> 00:11:10.330
traditional networking complex
topology versus a company that

00:11:10.330 --> 00:11:13.720
is Cloud native, has a
service in the Cloud already,

00:11:13.720 --> 00:11:16.960
and they can kind of get up
and running more quickly there?

00:11:16.960 --> 00:11:17.710
RYAN PRZBYL: Yeah.

00:11:17.710 --> 00:11:19.300
So I think the path--

00:11:19.300 --> 00:11:22.340
and I'm going to go back
in history a little bit.

00:11:22.340 --> 00:11:24.760
So four to five
years ago, I would

00:11:24.760 --> 00:11:27.440
say a lot of
conversations were, we

00:11:27.440 --> 00:11:28.690
want to dabble with the Cloud.

00:11:28.690 --> 00:11:30.820
We want to move some
applications in,

00:11:30.820 --> 00:11:32.832
nothing business critical.

00:11:32.832 --> 00:11:34.540
I see a lot of the
big organizations that

00:11:34.540 --> 00:11:35.380
are still trying to onboard.

00:11:35.380 --> 00:11:37.000
They're still kind
of in that model.

00:11:37.000 --> 00:11:39.880
Let's try to rebuild as
much of the data center

00:11:39.880 --> 00:11:42.707
as we can in the Cloud.

00:11:42.707 --> 00:11:43.540
And I use the term--

00:11:43.540 --> 00:11:45.475
I like fruit.

00:11:45.475 --> 00:11:48.700
Call it apples to apples
or apples to oranges.

00:11:48.700 --> 00:11:50.560
An apple is round and maybe red.

00:11:50.560 --> 00:11:54.400
And an orange is round
but maybe orange.

00:11:54.400 --> 00:11:55.900
We're trying to get
them to maybe go

00:11:55.900 --> 00:11:56.920
from apples to oranges.

00:11:56.920 --> 00:11:59.420
We don't want to get them to
go from apples to cherries.

00:11:59.420 --> 00:12:00.670
That's a very different fruit.

00:12:00.670 --> 00:12:03.520
It's much smaller, a
complete different color.

00:12:03.520 --> 00:12:05.830
Forgive my high-level
analogy there, but hopefully

00:12:05.830 --> 00:12:08.217
that makes sense.

00:12:08.217 --> 00:12:10.300
We want them to take some
advantages of the Cloud,

00:12:10.300 --> 00:12:13.390
but asking them to make
too much of a change

00:12:13.390 --> 00:12:16.780
is just more than some of these
large enterprises can bite off.

00:12:16.780 --> 00:12:18.760
You don't want to set
yourself on a path

00:12:18.760 --> 00:12:20.890
to migrate 10,000
things and then find out

00:12:20.890 --> 00:12:22.930
you changed so many
things when you migrated

00:12:22.930 --> 00:12:24.970
to Cloud that have the
applications don't work,

00:12:24.970 --> 00:12:26.590
they're broken.

00:12:26.590 --> 00:12:28.570
You can create a lot of
operational challenges

00:12:28.570 --> 00:12:29.272
for yourself.

00:12:29.272 --> 00:12:30.730
And that's where
our team comes in.

00:12:30.730 --> 00:12:32.380
We're trying to
really understand--

00:12:32.380 --> 00:12:33.640
while we're building
networks, we're

00:12:33.640 --> 00:12:35.440
trying to understand, what
are the applications that run

00:12:35.440 --> 00:12:36.670
on top of all of this stuff?

00:12:36.670 --> 00:12:38.830
Because we don't just
build networks in a vacuum.

00:12:38.830 --> 00:12:41.380
We build them to
support applications.

00:12:41.380 --> 00:12:45.820
But we also understand we're not
just dealing with technology.

00:12:45.820 --> 00:12:48.280
I can build all sorts of
networks in the Cloud.

00:12:48.280 --> 00:12:51.580
But you've got things
like operational models.

00:12:51.580 --> 00:12:54.700
There might be a team of 500
operational engineers who

00:12:54.700 --> 00:12:56.270
have to operate this
thing every day.

00:12:56.270 --> 00:12:58.522
And if they operate a
certain type of network,

00:12:58.522 --> 00:13:00.230
that's what they're
used to dealing with.

00:13:00.230 --> 00:13:03.770
And if I take that network and
completely change it too much,

00:13:03.770 --> 00:13:05.560
well, they got to
retrain 500 people.

00:13:05.560 --> 00:13:07.660
Their tools that they
use for monitoring

00:13:07.660 --> 00:13:09.010
might have to change.

00:13:09.010 --> 00:13:11.080
So it's not just
about the technology

00:13:11.080 --> 00:13:13.852
and how we're using it, but
you've got to really consider

00:13:13.852 --> 00:13:15.310
the applications
that we're moving,

00:13:15.310 --> 00:13:17.710
the operational models,
the business structure

00:13:17.710 --> 00:13:19.060
that the customer uses.

00:13:19.060 --> 00:13:21.190
And again, this is where
things get really complex

00:13:21.190 --> 00:13:23.140
with these large enterprises.

00:13:23.140 --> 00:13:24.190
They're businesses.

00:13:24.190 --> 00:13:26.050
They may have 30
different business units

00:13:26.050 --> 00:13:28.240
that all operate in
a very specific way.

00:13:28.240 --> 00:13:30.970
If they change things too
much and the business units

00:13:30.970 --> 00:13:32.590
can't operate in
that same way, that

00:13:32.590 --> 00:13:34.382
can be very problematic
for their business.

00:13:34.382 --> 00:13:37.360
So it's very interesting working
with these large enterprises

00:13:37.360 --> 00:13:39.910
because it's not purely a
technology conversation.

00:13:39.910 --> 00:13:41.577
We're trying to build
the right network,

00:13:41.577 --> 00:13:43.860
but we're trying to look at
all these different lenses

00:13:43.860 --> 00:13:46.360
to build the right network for
the customer that allows them

00:13:46.360 --> 00:13:49.000
to migrate in the Cloud,
capitalize on some of what

00:13:49.000 --> 00:13:51.730
the Cloud brings to
them, but not create

00:13:51.730 --> 00:13:54.640
so much change that it's
going to be completely

00:13:54.640 --> 00:13:55.725
disruptive for them.

00:13:55.725 --> 00:13:57.850
And that's where I think
the conversation is really

00:13:57.850 --> 00:13:59.950
interesting between the service
model and the architecture

00:13:59.950 --> 00:14:00.520
model.

00:14:00.520 --> 00:14:04.150
The service model can be
a very different change

00:14:04.150 --> 00:14:05.410
to start thinking about--

00:14:05.410 --> 00:14:06.040
think about this way.

00:14:06.040 --> 00:14:08.080
If you're a network engineer and
you're dealing with networking

00:14:08.080 --> 00:14:09.430
and routing and all of a
sudden they ask you to, say,

00:14:09.430 --> 00:14:10.810
don't be so
concerned about that.

00:14:10.810 --> 00:14:13.217
Be concerned more with how
your service is functioning.

00:14:13.217 --> 00:14:15.550
They're like, well, I've never
thought about my service.

00:14:15.550 --> 00:14:17.140
I've never really thought
about that before.

00:14:17.140 --> 00:14:18.760
I just built the infrastructure
and the service teams

00:14:18.760 --> 00:14:19.552
come in and use it.

00:14:19.552 --> 00:14:20.920
And I'm saying, no, no, no.

00:14:20.920 --> 00:14:23.680
We're going to do a
paradigm shift here.

00:14:23.680 --> 00:14:25.820
That can be a lot for
a company to bite off.

00:14:25.820 --> 00:14:27.887
So even though I think
that's the future--

00:14:27.887 --> 00:14:29.470
a lot of companies
are going that way.

00:14:29.470 --> 00:14:30.910
And that's even how Google runs.

00:14:30.910 --> 00:14:33.700
When you look at Google as
a company, we run services.

00:14:33.700 --> 00:14:34.360
We run Gmail.

00:14:34.360 --> 00:14:35.080
We run Search.

00:14:35.080 --> 00:14:36.190
We run YouTube.

00:14:36.190 --> 00:14:39.430
While we have a very
complex underlying network,

00:14:39.430 --> 00:14:42.400
that's not what we
focus on every day.

00:14:42.400 --> 00:14:43.750
We focus on the service.

00:14:43.750 --> 00:14:44.530
Is the service up?

00:14:44.530 --> 00:14:46.013
Is the service running?

00:14:46.013 --> 00:14:46.930
STEPHANIE WONG: Right.

00:14:46.930 --> 00:14:49.030
And this path that
we're talking about

00:14:49.030 --> 00:14:51.442
involves responsibility
both from the Cloud provider

00:14:51.442 --> 00:14:52.900
and the customer,
as you're saying.

00:14:52.900 --> 00:14:54.820
It's a paradigm shift for both.

00:14:54.820 --> 00:14:57.610
So when we're talking about
building a bridge between going

00:14:57.610 --> 00:15:01.060
from apples to oranges, what
is actually being abstracted

00:15:01.060 --> 00:15:02.980
and handled by a
provider like Google

00:15:02.980 --> 00:15:05.182
Cloud in a
service-oriented model?

00:15:05.182 --> 00:15:07.140
RYAN PRZBYL: Yeah, it's
a really good question.

00:15:07.140 --> 00:15:09.367
So if you look at a
traditional networking model

00:15:09.367 --> 00:15:11.950
and I have a bunch of different
applications that are running,

00:15:11.950 --> 00:15:14.987
just as an example,
typically you were saying,

00:15:14.987 --> 00:15:16.570
this application is
running over here,

00:15:16.570 --> 00:15:17.710
and this application
is running over here,

00:15:17.710 --> 00:15:19.190
and the two need
to talk together.

00:15:19.190 --> 00:15:20.935
So what is the route
to get between them?

00:15:20.935 --> 00:15:23.560
And what sort of functionality,
what sort of security parameter

00:15:23.560 --> 00:15:25.780
do I have to overlay
on the top of it?

00:15:25.780 --> 00:15:28.920
But generally speaking,
we're dealing with layer 3

00:15:28.920 --> 00:15:32.520
and 4 connectivity
with a security

00:15:32.520 --> 00:15:34.660
bend to it at some level.

00:15:34.660 --> 00:15:36.660
When you start to talk
about service networking,

00:15:36.660 --> 00:15:38.368
let me talk about one
particular product.

00:15:38.368 --> 00:15:41.640
We talk about private service
connect, or PSC as we call it.

00:15:41.640 --> 00:15:44.730
PSC is designed to be what we
would-- the term I would use

00:15:44.730 --> 00:15:46.590
is a service endpoint.

00:15:46.590 --> 00:15:49.740
So think about it as an endpoint
that you put into your network

00:15:49.740 --> 00:15:51.117
that front ends a service.

00:15:51.117 --> 00:15:52.950
And that could be a
service you're consuming

00:15:52.950 --> 00:15:54.428
from some outside entity.

00:15:54.428 --> 00:15:56.220
It could be a service
that you're consuming

00:15:56.220 --> 00:15:57.960
from another business unit.

00:15:57.960 --> 00:16:00.840
But it abstracts a
lot of things for you

00:16:00.840 --> 00:16:03.063
and changes the
conversation to, how do I

00:16:03.063 --> 00:16:04.230
route between here and here?

00:16:04.230 --> 00:16:06.120
It's kind of just, how do
I get to the end point?

00:16:06.120 --> 00:16:07.495
But once it gets
to the endpoint,

00:16:07.495 --> 00:16:10.470
PSC does a lot of things under
the covers that you don't see.

00:16:10.470 --> 00:16:13.170
It handles the
routing to that other,

00:16:13.170 --> 00:16:15.420
what we call the service
producer, which, again, could

00:16:15.420 --> 00:16:16.837
be something that
lives completely

00:16:16.837 --> 00:16:18.150
outside of your organization.

00:16:18.150 --> 00:16:19.470
Could be a third party
company that you're

00:16:19.470 --> 00:16:20.610
buying a service from.

00:16:20.610 --> 00:16:22.620
So how do you route
to that company?

00:16:22.620 --> 00:16:24.960
How do you handle
overlapping IP space?

00:16:24.960 --> 00:16:26.880
If you're a traditional
network engineer

00:16:26.880 --> 00:16:28.170
and you're looking at
this, you're kind of going,

00:16:28.170 --> 00:16:30.600
OK, I've got this IP space
here and this IP space here

00:16:30.600 --> 00:16:32.520
and I have to manage
it in some way

00:16:32.520 --> 00:16:34.920
or I have to control
NADing between them,

00:16:34.920 --> 00:16:37.680
and I'm going to install this
device that's going to do this.

00:16:37.680 --> 00:16:39.990
Again, very network-centric
conversation.

00:16:39.990 --> 00:16:42.090
You introduce PSC to
the mix, and it's like,

00:16:42.090 --> 00:16:43.830
just route it to the
end point and Google

00:16:43.830 --> 00:16:44.997
will manage everything else.

00:16:44.997 --> 00:16:48.030
We'll manage the routing to
somebody else in somebody

00:16:48.030 --> 00:16:49.510
else's environment completely.

00:16:49.510 --> 00:16:52.060
We'll handle all the NADing
and all of that stuff for you

00:16:52.060 --> 00:16:53.560
so you don't have
to worry about it.

00:16:53.560 --> 00:16:55.560
So again, it just
becomes this endpoint

00:16:55.560 --> 00:16:59.250
that sits in your network
that front ends the service.

00:16:59.250 --> 00:17:01.650
And we're looking at doing
that even with Google APIs

00:17:01.650 --> 00:17:04.890
and Google Services where you
can put a PSC endpoint in place

00:17:04.890 --> 00:17:06.540
to front end those APIs.

00:17:06.540 --> 00:17:08.550
And you can control it
a little bit different.

00:17:08.550 --> 00:17:10.079
Now, one of the
things I would say

00:17:10.079 --> 00:17:12.599
is if you're a
traditional network team

00:17:12.599 --> 00:17:14.680
and you're like,
well, OK, what happens

00:17:14.680 --> 00:17:15.930
when the service doesn't work?

00:17:15.930 --> 00:17:18.012
How do I troubleshoot this?

00:17:18.012 --> 00:17:20.220
Because Google is managing
a bunch of the [INAUDIBLE]

00:17:20.220 --> 00:17:21.720
for you, Google's managing
some of the routing,

00:17:21.720 --> 00:17:23.678
Google's managing some
NADing under the covers,

00:17:23.678 --> 00:17:25.829
you kind of lose visibility
to some of that stuff.

00:17:25.829 --> 00:17:28.830
And if you're thinking for a
very network-centric mindset,

00:17:28.830 --> 00:17:30.570
it kind of causes this
knee jerk reaction

00:17:30.570 --> 00:17:33.930
to say like, OK, as a network
engineer myself, somebody who's

00:17:33.930 --> 00:17:35.430
been in the situation
of like, is it

00:17:35.430 --> 00:17:37.140
the network that's
causing the problem,

00:17:37.140 --> 00:17:39.360
the network engineers are always
trying to clear the network

00:17:39.360 --> 00:17:40.568
and say it's not the network.

00:17:40.568 --> 00:17:42.880
And you need
visibility end to end.

00:17:42.880 --> 00:17:44.190
But with something like PSC--

00:17:44.190 --> 00:17:45.780
and I'm not saying
PSC is a bad thing,

00:17:45.780 --> 00:17:48.540
but once it hits that point, you
kind of lose visibility to it.

00:17:48.540 --> 00:17:49.980
Is it something on
the other side that's

00:17:49.980 --> 00:17:51.188
not working that you can see?

00:17:51.188 --> 00:17:53.625
Is it the service producer
that's having a problem?

00:17:53.625 --> 00:17:55.500
At the end of the day,
what really matters is

00:17:55.500 --> 00:17:57.690
the service is down.

00:17:57.690 --> 00:17:59.850
But how you troubleshoot
it and how you

00:17:59.850 --> 00:18:01.433
approach it is very different.

00:18:01.433 --> 00:18:02.350
STEPHANIE WONG: Right.

00:18:02.350 --> 00:18:06.030
And I can understand hesitation
around relinquishing control

00:18:06.030 --> 00:18:09.270
or visibility so that you can
troubleshoot and actually find

00:18:09.270 --> 00:18:10.260
root of cause.

00:18:10.260 --> 00:18:13.260
And we'll talk more about
this later about some

00:18:13.260 --> 00:18:16.848
of the network intelligence
that we provide for visibility,

00:18:16.848 --> 00:18:19.140
but I do want to highlight
a point that you were making

00:18:19.140 --> 00:18:23.430
earlier about not needing to
completely shift your existing

00:18:23.430 --> 00:18:25.535
model to the Cloud when
it doesn't make sense.

00:18:25.535 --> 00:18:27.660
And it kind of reminds me
of what we tell customers

00:18:27.660 --> 00:18:29.460
about microservice
migration, people

00:18:29.460 --> 00:18:31.620
who are moving from
virtual machines

00:18:31.620 --> 00:18:34.050
into more of a
containerised environment.

00:18:34.050 --> 00:18:36.270
You should actually
move what makes sense.

00:18:36.270 --> 00:18:37.770
And you don't
necessarily need to do

00:18:37.770 --> 00:18:40.560
a complete decoupling
or re-architecture.

00:18:40.560 --> 00:18:43.170
Are you seeing most companies
using Cloud native apps

00:18:43.170 --> 00:18:46.140
to adopt this
service-oriented model?

00:18:46.140 --> 00:18:48.150
Or is there actually
an opportunity

00:18:48.150 --> 00:18:51.150
to use traditional more
hefty applications built

00:18:51.150 --> 00:18:53.910
on complex networking
models and shift that

00:18:53.910 --> 00:18:55.087
to a service-oriented model?

00:18:55.087 --> 00:18:56.920
RYAN PRZBYL: So it's a
really good question.

00:18:56.920 --> 00:18:59.400
I think the answer is you can.

00:18:59.400 --> 00:19:01.500
So if you have these big
monolithic applications--

00:19:01.500 --> 00:19:03.390
and we do talk to
customers about this--

00:19:03.390 --> 00:19:07.440
you can spend time to
re-architect the application.

00:19:07.440 --> 00:19:10.080
The question is, do you want to?

00:19:10.080 --> 00:19:12.210
So if it's an
application that is going

00:19:12.210 --> 00:19:14.100
to be business critical
for the next 20 years

00:19:14.100 --> 00:19:17.670
and you're wanting to move into
more of a service-centric model

00:19:17.670 --> 00:19:19.710
and you want to break it
down into microservices

00:19:19.710 --> 00:19:22.085
so you can make changes to
small parts of the application

00:19:22.085 --> 00:19:24.420
without trying to overhaul
the whole thing, sure.

00:19:24.420 --> 00:19:26.612
It may make sense
for you to do that.

00:19:26.612 --> 00:19:28.320
But think about it
from this perspective.

00:19:28.320 --> 00:19:30.335
You have a working application.

00:19:30.335 --> 00:19:32.460
Maybe it doesn't really
work with the service level

00:19:32.460 --> 00:19:33.090
constructs.

00:19:33.090 --> 00:19:35.550
It's not set up
as a microservice.

00:19:35.550 --> 00:19:38.370
It is just this big monolithic
thing that you want to move.

00:19:38.370 --> 00:19:41.380
We move those things all the
time, and they work just fine.

00:19:41.380 --> 00:19:43.800
So if you're a business
leader in that company

00:19:43.800 --> 00:19:46.870
and you can migrate that
application into the Cloud,

00:19:46.870 --> 00:19:50.730
a lot of times it's, just do
it and don't spend the time

00:19:50.730 --> 00:19:53.130
and the resources to
re-architect the whole thing

00:19:53.130 --> 00:19:54.720
because a lot of
these applications--

00:19:54.720 --> 00:19:55.650
they're not going
to be something

00:19:55.650 --> 00:19:56.970
you're in use for 20 years.

00:19:56.970 --> 00:19:58.145
They're going to change.

00:19:58.145 --> 00:20:00.270
So again, I'll go back to
that term technical debt.

00:20:00.270 --> 00:20:01.950
You could move a very
large application

00:20:01.950 --> 00:20:03.900
in and it brings with
it some technical debt.

00:20:03.900 --> 00:20:05.760
You could spend a
lot of time and money

00:20:05.760 --> 00:20:07.560
trying to fix a
lot of that stuff.

00:20:07.560 --> 00:20:09.000
Or you can just
take the approach

00:20:09.000 --> 00:20:11.700
of, I know in five years that
application is going to go away

00:20:11.700 --> 00:20:14.190
anyways, so I don't want to
spend the time and the money

00:20:14.190 --> 00:20:17.460
to refactor, re-architect
the application

00:20:17.460 --> 00:20:19.440
into a new construct.

00:20:19.440 --> 00:20:22.080
I just know when we build the
next one or whatever that next

00:20:22.080 --> 00:20:24.280
application is for that
piece of my business--

00:20:24.280 --> 00:20:26.660
maybe it's your
HR as an example--

00:20:26.660 --> 00:20:29.823
it's going to be more
Cloud native in the future.

00:20:29.823 --> 00:20:32.240
Maybe we're going to go and
use Software-as-a-Service from

00:20:32.240 --> 00:20:34.880
somebody else, and this is going
to be a service that's running

00:20:34.880 --> 00:20:35.810
in the Cloud.

00:20:35.810 --> 00:20:39.440
And maybe that company is going
to use PSC, as an example,

00:20:39.440 --> 00:20:44.000
to basically put an endpoint
into my network that basically

00:20:44.000 --> 00:20:47.210
means my entire monolithic
application that I use today is

00:20:47.210 --> 00:20:49.550
going to go away in five
years because I'm now using

00:20:49.550 --> 00:20:52.680
Software-as-a-Service
through some other company.

00:20:52.680 --> 00:20:55.250
So when you think about
that, does it really

00:20:55.250 --> 00:20:58.130
make sense to spend the time
and the effort to do it?

00:20:58.130 --> 00:21:01.340
In a lot of cases, my point
of view is it doesn't.

00:21:01.340 --> 00:21:04.340
We can make it work in
the Cloud most times.

00:21:04.340 --> 00:21:08.615
So let's make it work, and
let's just move on and just

00:21:08.615 --> 00:21:10.490
acknowledge that there's
technical debt there

00:21:10.490 --> 00:21:13.580
and it maybe is not the optimal
architecture for what you want.

00:21:13.580 --> 00:21:13.880
STEPHANIE WONG: Right.

00:21:13.880 --> 00:21:16.070
And I think companies
probably are on the same page,

00:21:16.070 --> 00:21:21.170
as you not wanting to reinvest
a whole lot of upfront capital

00:21:21.170 --> 00:21:23.900
just to change the things
that are already working.

00:21:23.900 --> 00:21:25.800
And as we know,
architectural changes

00:21:25.800 --> 00:21:29.660
beget new security reviews and
a lot of downstream effects.

00:21:29.660 --> 00:21:33.410
So do you have any stories
of customers or people

00:21:33.410 --> 00:21:35.930
you've worked with that have
had to go through this process?

00:21:35.930 --> 00:21:36.680
RYAN PRZBYL: Yeah.

00:21:36.680 --> 00:21:39.562
So interesting enough, I was
working with one customer.

00:21:39.562 --> 00:21:42.020
And one of the things people
have to understand about Cloud

00:21:42.020 --> 00:21:43.682
is it's a constantly
evolving model.

00:21:43.682 --> 00:21:45.890
And I think we'll talk about
this in the next segment

00:21:45.890 --> 00:21:47.240
that we're going to do.

00:21:47.240 --> 00:21:49.430
It's not like your
traditional data center.

00:21:49.430 --> 00:21:51.260
Things change much
faster in the Cloud.

00:21:51.260 --> 00:21:53.960
And we'll talk about
that, of the why.

00:21:53.960 --> 00:21:56.750
But a lot of enterprises-- to
change their architecture--

00:21:56.750 --> 00:21:59.600
I actually had a very
senior architect kind

00:21:59.600 --> 00:22:02.420
of yelling at me on the
phone and kind of saying,

00:22:02.420 --> 00:22:05.220
it's great that Google can
shift on the fly like that.

00:22:05.220 --> 00:22:07.342
But every time we make
architectural changes,

00:22:07.342 --> 00:22:09.050
they have to go through
security reviews.

00:22:09.050 --> 00:22:11.550
There's all of this red
tape that has to be done.

00:22:11.550 --> 00:22:12.770
So when we think about--

00:22:12.770 --> 00:22:14.270
with this particular
customer, I was

00:22:14.270 --> 00:22:16.760
like, this is the way the
architecture will look today.

00:22:16.760 --> 00:22:18.650
In 6 months or 12
months, there's

00:22:18.650 --> 00:22:20.060
all these other features are
going to come down the pipe.

00:22:20.060 --> 00:22:22.080
And this is how I would
change it in the future.

00:22:22.080 --> 00:22:25.453
And that's where it was creating
this really tough rub to say,

00:22:25.453 --> 00:22:27.620
yeah, but for me to implement
those changes is going

00:22:27.620 --> 00:22:29.090
to take me 6 to 12
months because I

00:22:29.090 --> 00:22:30.632
have to have security
reviews, I have

00:22:30.632 --> 00:22:32.000
to have architecture reviews.

00:22:32.000 --> 00:22:35.060
So there was all
this red tape that

00:22:35.060 --> 00:22:38.550
comes around with making
these changes to customers.

00:22:38.550 --> 00:22:41.120
So while we're always saying
like, the Cloud is constantly

00:22:41.120 --> 00:22:42.995
evolving, you want to
constantly be changing,

00:22:42.995 --> 00:22:45.860
we also have to be
cognizant that Google

00:22:45.860 --> 00:22:46.830
can change on a dime.

00:22:46.830 --> 00:22:49.100
This is the way
Google was built.

00:22:49.100 --> 00:22:50.750
But not all enterprises
can do that.

00:22:50.750 --> 00:22:52.010
And that's OK.

00:22:52.010 --> 00:22:53.510
I think we as a
company just have

00:22:53.510 --> 00:22:56.480
to be cognizant that not
everybody operates at Google.

00:22:56.480 --> 00:22:57.980
And I say it's kind
of a funny story

00:22:57.980 --> 00:22:59.720
because literally they
were yelling at me saying,

00:22:59.720 --> 00:23:01.580
I have to go back to
enterprise 101 school

00:23:01.580 --> 00:23:03.443
because that's not
how enterprises work.

00:23:03.443 --> 00:23:04.360
STEPHANIE WONG: Right.

00:23:04.360 --> 00:23:04.860
Right.

00:23:04.860 --> 00:23:07.670
And I do want to dig into
this a lot more, Ryan,

00:23:07.670 --> 00:23:10.310
but we're going to save some
of this for the next segment,

00:23:10.310 --> 00:23:11.690
as you mentioned.

00:23:11.690 --> 00:23:13.190
And I want to dive
a little bit more

00:23:13.190 --> 00:23:17.540
into that operational shift and
what Google Cloud specifically

00:23:17.540 --> 00:23:19.820
is doing to build
a bridge to get

00:23:19.820 --> 00:23:22.850
people to have a stepping stone
into a service-oriented model.

00:23:22.850 --> 00:23:25.160
So Ryan, thank you so
much for joining today.

00:23:25.160 --> 00:23:27.330
And for everybody
else, stay tuned.

00:23:27.330 --> 00:23:29.690
We're going to come out with
another episode shortly.

00:23:29.690 --> 00:23:32.340
RYAN PRZBYL: Thanks for
having me, Stephanie.

00:23:32.340 --> 00:23:35.990
[MUSIC PLAYING]

